1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
The race for New York's 23rd congressional district.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,000
The Iran war.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Fuel prices.

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Immigration.

5
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
The issues voters say matter most in this election.

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Tonight, those issues taking center stage

7
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,000
as incumbent Nick Langworthy faces off

8
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,000
against challenger Dr. Aaron Geese

9
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
on the 18 news debate stage.

10
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Live from your local election headquarters.

11
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
This is debate night in New York.

12
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Good evening, everyone.

13
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Thank you for joining us for tonight's debate

14
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,000
between the two candidates running for the New York House

15
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
District 23 race in New York,

16
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
running for the U.S. House of Representatives.

17
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,000
My name is Jim Turpin.

18
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
I'm the news director at WETM TV 18 News,

19
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,000
and I'll be your moderator tonight.

20
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,000
The candidates were provided the rules for the debates

21
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:50,000
several weeks ago.

22
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
No prepared notes are allowed on the candidates' podiums

23
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:54,000
and they do not have them.

24
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,000
I will ask questions of the candidates in alternate order.

25
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,000
The candidates will have one minute to reply,

26
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:03,000
and 30 seconds to rebut if they want to.

27
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Each candidate will then have 25 seconds

28
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
if they choose to rebut the rebuttal.

29
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,000
So anyway, we'll see how that goes.

30
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
They will hear the questions for the first time

31
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:14,000
as I ask of them.

32
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,000
They have not been provided the questions in advance.

33
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,000
If they go a little long on their answers,

34
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
they will hear this sound,

35
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,000
and then they'll know to stop.

36
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:24,000
We hope we don't have to use that tonight.

37
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,000
The debate is live on WETM TV, WETM TV+,

38
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,000
also on mytwintears.com, and WIVB+, in Buffalo.

39
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
And I want to thank everybody for watching on that platform.

40
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
My first question tonight goes to you, Congressman Langworthy.

41
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Old school questions tonight on paper.

42
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,000
What do you consider the most important issue

43
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
in this election for the people of New York District 23?

44
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:49,000
I think the most important issue on everybody's mind

45
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,000
is the question of the affordability.

46
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,000
And we have had very stubborn inflation these last five years.

47
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Larry Summers, a liberal economics professor

48
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
that was an adviser to the Clinton administration,

49
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,000
said that if the government spends trillions of dollars

50
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,000
that it wasn't budgeted for,

51
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,000
we're going to have inflation that's going to last for some time.

52
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,000
We're in that period.

53
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Government doesn't set prices.

54
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Unfortunately, we have to grow our way out of this,

55
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:23,000
and that's why I'm very proud that we have provided the tax relief

56
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,000
that we have to put more money in people's pockets as they face this.

57
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
I mean, and we've cut taxes on tips over time.

58
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
We've given no tax on Social Security for our middle-class seniors.

59
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Having that money available to them is critically important.

60
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Dr. Geese, same question to you.

61
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,000
What do you consider the most important issue

62
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,000
for voters in New York District 23?

63
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Well, Jim, there's no question that it is affordability.

64
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,000
But the affordability of what?

65
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Folks are concerned about their utility bills.

66
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,000
They're concerned about the prices for everyday goods, for groceries.

67
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,000
They're concerned about the cost of gas.

68
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
And most of all, what I hear on the campaign trail,

69
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,000
and I'm in the midst of a 100-town hall tour,

70
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
just did our 60th last night,

71
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
is folks are concerned about their health care.

72
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
They don't know how they're going to pay for their insurance premiums,

73
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,000
how they're going to afford to keep their relative in a nursing home,

74
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
how they're going to afford to, if they get sick.

75
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,000
So to get back to everyday prices,

76
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,000
gas prices are high in the United States right now.

77
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
They're about $4.50 in the district today,

78
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:26,000
and diesel is even higher.

79
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Diesel is at record highs.

80
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,000
And we should be clear about why that is.

81
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:32,000
It's because of President Trump's war in Iran,

82
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,000
when President Trump ran on no more wars.

83
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Inflation.

84
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Inflation is also due to the big, ugly bill,

85
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
which adds $4.5 trillion to our national debt

86
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,000
for a tax cut for wealthy corporations.

87
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Okay, Dr. Geese, the next question goes to you,

88
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
and you're kind of talking about it right now.

89
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
The war against Iran continues.

90
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,000
In your opinion, is it time for the U.S. to end the war,

91
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
or should it continue to a conclusion acceptable

92
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
to President Trump and his cabinet?

93
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Well, Jim, first of all, it's not clear what that acceptable end would be.

94
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,000
The administration continues to change the answer

95
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,000
when they're asked what their strategic objectives are.

96
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
But the American people can't wait.

97
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:19,000
This war is making our lives unlivable.

98
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
When the price of diesel goes above $6 a gallon,

99
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,000
everything that we buy, everything that comes on a truck costs more.

100
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Getting to work costs more, and so wages mean less.

101
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Meanwhile, the Strait of Hormuz remains closed,

102
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
and there is no plan except, apparently, to keep on bombing.

103
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Yes, in my view, on day one, the next Congress

104
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
should pass a binding war powers resolution

105
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,000
that would compel the president to wind up the conflict

106
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,000
in a dignified way within 40 days,

107
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
and then we need to work with our partners

108
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:57,000
to achieve our strategic objectives through negotiation with Iran.

109
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Congressman Langworthy, same question.

110
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Should the war end now, or should it continue

111
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,000
until it reaches the conclusion that the president would like?

112
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
I would like the war in Iran to end at the earliest possible date.

113
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
But you don't tie the commander in chief's hands.

114
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,000
We have one commander in chief at a time.

115
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,000
I didn't make a decision to go to war,

116
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,000
but we find ourselves in that position now.

117
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
It has certainly driven up the cost of gasoline and diesel fuel,

118
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
but at the same time, the objective to prevent

119
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:32,000
the Islamic regime in Iran from having a nuclear weapon

120
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,000
is extraordinarily important,

121
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:38,000
because they would use it.

122
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:42,000
This is the largest state sponsor of terror the world has ever known.

123
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,000
They have the blood of countless nations under their fingernails,

124
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
including American citizens.

125
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,000
They have said that they wanted to negotiate an end.

126
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,000
We had diplomats on planes on our way there

127
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
only for them to move the goal post and change.

128
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
This regime has notoriously lied.

129
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
We have to get to a diplomatic solution.

130
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:02,000
I don't support ground troops at this time going to Iran.

131
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,000
I don't believe that that is the will of the country.

132
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
But we cannot tie the hands of the commander in chief.

133
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,000
I'd like to respond to that briefly, if I may.

134
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Is this a rebuttal question?

135
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
It can be.

136
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:21,000
It would not be the Congress tying the hands of the president.

137
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:25,000
The Constitution ties the hands of the American president,

138
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
because it clearly guarantees to Congress the right

139
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:30,000
to declare war and to negotiate peace,

140
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
and it is high time that the Congress reclaimed that right

141
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,000
on behalf of the American people,

142
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
because the objectives in this conflict are not clear,

143
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
and this war is costing American lives, American treasure.

144
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
It is causing the lives of people in other nations affecting their security,

145
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
and it must end with a negotiated settlement

146
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
if it doesn't end with ground troops.

147
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
And President Langworthy is correct that we should not invade on the ground.

148
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,000
Congressman, would you like to respond?

149
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,000
It's rather naive to suggest that 435 members of Congress

150
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:02,000
are going to negotiate with the Iranian regime on an end to the war.

151
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,000
And if you tell a regime like this that has sponsored terror

152
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,000
through its proxies across the globe,

153
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
that you're going to be out in 45 days,

154
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
you're going to have the same kind of impact

155
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,000
that we had with the botched withdrawal from Afghanistan.

156
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:18,000
My next question comes from the Steuben and Shimon counties

157
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,000
chapter of the League of Women Voters.

158
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:21,000
Congressman, this one goes to you.

159
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
Are elections in the United States safe and fair?

160
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:28,000
I fight for voter ID, and I voted for it many times.

161
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,000
Unfortunately, we can't find bipartisan collaboration on that,

162
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:37,000
despite the fact that 80 percent of the public wants and demands it.

163
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:41,000
We have 10 million people come under over our borders illegally,

164
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
many of which we have no idea why they're here.

165
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
Showing your identification is something very simple.

166
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:51,000
You have to use it for many transactions across American life.

167
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
This is a routine thing that we do.

168
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,000
Yet there's such an intense allergy on the other side

169
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,000
to ask for identification in our elections.

170
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:01,000
That's the perfect way to get any guesswork out of our elections,

171
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:05,000
is to have the voter ID standard be across all 50 states.

172
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,000
Same question to you, Dr. Giese, from the Steuben and Shimon counties chapter

173
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:09,000
of the League of Women Voters.

174
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Are elections in the United States safe and fair?

175
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:15,000
I believe they are, and I would love to keep them that way.

176
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,000
I think one of the best ways to do that is to keep our federal government

177
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,000
from meddling in elections, which again,

178
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:25,000
per the Constitution, are reserved to the states.

179
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,000
Now, when it comes to voter ID, I don't support the Save America Act,

180
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:31,000
but I do support voter ID.

181
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:34,000
I don't know if Congressman Langworthy has ever worked as a poll worker,

182
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:38,000
but I have, and I know that when you sign in at the polls,

183
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:41,000
and when you do this in a few weeks when it's time to vote,

184
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,000
there's a bipartisan team, and they're there to verify your identity.

185
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:48,000
When you register to vote at the BOE or the DMV or wherever you do that,

186
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:51,000
you have to show ID, and that should be the standard.

187
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
But the Save America Act would mean that, say,

188
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:56,000
folks on large reservations who only have their tribal ID,

189
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,000
that ID would not be valid for them to vote.

190
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:02,000
Folks who don't happen to have a passport or a marriage certificate

191
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:05,000
that shows their married name, they wouldn't be able to vote.

192
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:08,000
And voting is not like being in a private club.

193
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,000
It is a right, guaranteed to us by the Constitution.

194
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:14,000
We have to make it possible for every citizen above the age of 18 to vote.

195
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:17,000
And you should simply be who you say that you are

196
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:20,000
and show an identification to prove that that's who exactly

197
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
is signing in at that polling place.

198
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:26,000
This is not a tall order to ask anyone to produce it.

199
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:29,000
I mean, you need to use ID all the time.

200
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:33,000
Not every state has the same standards of bipartisan poll workers.

201
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
So the state of Georgia, up until their reforms,

202
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:39,000
had absolutely no bipartisanship in the past.

203
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:43,000
You know, these things need to be standard and uniform.

204
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,000
Voter identification is a common-sense solution.

205
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
All right, Dr. Geese, next question goes to you.

206
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,000
This question also comes from the League of Women Voters.

207
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:55,000
Millions of Americans are suffering, rather, financially

208
00:09:55,000 --> 00:10:00,000
due to the high price of gas, groceries, and other goods needed for daily life.

209
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:03,000
What policies would you support that would lower these prices?

210
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
Certainly.

211
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:10,000
So there are a number of important things that we could do, and I think do quickly.

212
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,000
The first we've already talked about, and that is,

213
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
we need to wind up the conflict with Iran,

214
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:19,000
which we both agree is driving up the cost of gas and diesel,

215
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
and that is driving up the cost of everything,

216
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:26,000
because pretty much everything comes to us on a truck or a boat.

217
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:30,000
The second thing that we need to do is reverse President Trump's tariffs.

218
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:35,000
Consumers have already paid an average of $1,700 last year because of the tariffs.

219
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:40,000
And in this district, the tariffs particularly hurt our relationship with Canada,

220
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:44,000
which is a really important economic and personal relationship

221
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
that we have here in western New York and the southern tier.

222
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:50,000
Border crossings were down last year by 25 percent,

223
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:54,000
and I don't think they'll be up this year based on this new phase of the trade war.

224
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
So that will help to bring prices down.

225
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,000
It will help to give a boost to small business,

226
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:03,000
which is disproportionately hurt by these arbitrary, job-killing tariffs.

227
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
Congressman Langworthy, same question to you.

228
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
Millions of Americans are suffering financially

229
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
due to the high price of gas, groceries, and other goods needed for everyday life.

230
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:14,000
What policies would you support to try to bring these down?

231
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:18,000
Well, one of the first things we did is we lowered taxes across the board

232
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:21,000
so that our small businesses can grow and thrive and employ people

233
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:26,000
as they do, you know, so many small businesses I visit all over this district.

234
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
My opponent attacks the tariffs.

235
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:32,000
The tariff is the most pro-worker tool that can be in the arsenal.

236
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:36,000
We have sold out our jobs across western New York and the southern tier

237
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:39,000
through free trade agreements over the last 30 years,

238
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:44,000
and we could list factory after factory, business after business that's left here

239
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
and taking the jobs with them and then taking the families

240
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:51,000
as they've had to spread all over to find opportunity elsewhere.

241
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:54,000
We have to, in order to bring our gas prices down,

242
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,000
we need more refining capability in this country.

243
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:02,000
We are a net exporter of oil and fossil fuels.

244
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:05,000
We need to build more oil refineries here in the United States of America.

245
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
We haven't done it in 50 years,

246
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:11,000
whether it was the war in the Ukraine breaking out or war in the Middle East.

247
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:16,000
We shouldn't be dependent on global affairs on all of our gas and fuel prices.

248
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:17,000
If I may?

249
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,000
Sure.

250
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:23,000
The tax cuts in the big ugly bill overwhelmingly go to the wealthiest Americans

251
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
and the corporations that they control.

252
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
They are deficit tax cuts.

253
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
That is, they are on our nation's credit card.

254
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:32,000
They weaken workers, they weaken small business,

255
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:35,000
and they use the government to subsidize the richest people

256
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
in the history of the planet.

257
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
We are not set to grow ourselves out of this.

258
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
We are hurting small business.

259
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:42,000
We need to reverse the tariffs.

260
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
We need to reduce taxes on working people

261
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
and stop using the government to subsidize

262
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
the richest people who have ever existed.

263
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:51,000
We did reduce taxes on working people.

264
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
We doubled the child tax credit.

265
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
We gave no tax on Social Security for our seniors,

266
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
no tax on tips, no tax on overtime.

267
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
That isn't millionaires and billionaires.

268
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
That's regular people right here in our community

269
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:07,000
that have their tax refund checks went up 11% year over year.

270
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:08,000
That's a matter of fact.

271
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:09,000
It's indisputable.

272
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
There's more money in their pocket this year,

273
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:15,000
and it's not something that my opponent can dispute.

274
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:16,000
All right.

275
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,000
Congressman Langworthy, the next question starts with you.

276
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:21,000
Government projections show that Social Security Fund

277
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:24,000
could be insolvent by 2032 if something isn't done.

278
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
What do you think, if anything, should be done

279
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:31,000
to ensure that future retirees can still draw Social Security benefits?

280
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:34,000
I think it's time for us to have a Social Security commission

281
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
on a bipartisan basis.

282
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
This has been done in the past.

283
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:42,000
I mean, it's much like when you decide which bases are going

284
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
as you move the military bases around the country

285
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,000
and reposition them.

286
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:49,000
You cannot do this on a partisan basis.

287
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:50,000
It's far too serious.

288
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:53,000
Social Security is a contract with the American people.

289
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
You paid in all your life into this,

290
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,000
and so many people's livelihoods are dependent on it.

291
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:02,000
We have to have a modernization of Social Security

292
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:06,000
done in a bipartisan fashion, take the politics out of it,

293
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:10,000
and let's have a bright and vibrant future

294
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
while meeting that promise that has been made

295
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
to American workers their whole lives.

296
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:15,000
Dr. Gee, same question.

297
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:18,000
What should be done to keep Social Security solvent?

298
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
Well, it's difficult for me to tell

299
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,000
what Congressman Langworthy's preferred solution would be

300
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
other than that he wants it to be bipartisan,

301
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
which is certainly correct.

302
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
We have to do this in a bipartisan way.

303
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
But let me say this first.

304
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:34,000
We should never privatize Social Security,

305
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:38,000
which would turn it into a 401K and make it easier

306
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:41,000
for the government to steal from

307
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:44,000
or make it subject to market pressures.

308
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
So if that's the solution, count me out.

309
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:50,000
What we should do is eliminate the cap on Social Security earnings

310
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:54,000
so that if you earn more than $185,000 a year,

311
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:57,000
those earnings in excess of $185,000 a year

312
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
would also be subject to the Social Security tax.

313
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:03,000
This would not compel rich Americans to pay more proportionally

314
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
than working class and middle class Americans

315
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
just the same thing.

316
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
That is their fair share.

317
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:12,000
If we do that, then we can keep Social Security solvent

318
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
and meet our commitment to our seniors,

319
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:17,000
which I agree we must meet.

320
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
All right, we're going to take a short break,

321
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:23,000
and we'll be back with the debate on WETM+, WETM.

322
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:26,000
Thank you for watching.

323
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Okay, we're back with our U.S. House District 23

324
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
New York congressional debate.

325
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
Dr. Yeast, next question goes to you.

326
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
Former President Bill Clinton won an election

327
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
by campaigning with the slogan,

328
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
it's the economy, stupid.

329
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
Do you think the upcoming midterm election

330
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
is mostly about the economy?

331
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:56,000
I do. I agree that it's mostly about the economy.

332
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:00,000
I think that the stock market keeps going up,

333
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
and when I look at it, it makes me nervous

334
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:05,000
because I live here, and I see businesses closing,

335
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
and I see people out of work,

336
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:10,000
and I see that homelessness is up in the southern tier

337
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:13,000
by 32% almost last year,

338
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
according to the report by the Comptroller.

339
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:18,000
So folks out here, they're not doing well.

340
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
They're not better off than they were

341
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:24,000
a couple of years ago, and so we have to ask ourselves why,

342
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:27,000
and I believe one reason is that big ugly bill,

343
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
and another reason is the war,

344
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
and a third reason is the tariffs,

345
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,000
and I'm particularly concerned about what that big ugly bill

346
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:37,000
is about to do to our healthcare in early 2027

347
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:40,000
when the cuts to Medicare and Medicaid go into effect.

348
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:43,000
Already we're seeing nursing homes close.

349
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:46,000
We've lost five in the last couple of years.

350
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
We've lost 28 pharmacies.

351
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:51,000
One study showed that eight out of our 12 hospitals in the district

352
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:54,000
are in danger of closure if those cuts go into effect.

353
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
So yes, it's about the economy.

354
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
Representative Langworthy, same question to you.

355
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
Is it about the economy in this election?

356
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Every election in New York's 23rd District

357
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:05,000
and throughout Western New York, or upstate New York for that matter,

358
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
is about the economy,

359
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
and it's the economy that's been brought to us

360
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
by New York State government run amok

361
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
and Kathy Hochul's policies.

362
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
Your electric bill has doubled

363
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
as Kathy Hochul's public service commission

364
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:20,000
has raised rates 48 times.

365
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:24,000
They have found more ways to tax, regulate,

366
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
and nickel and dime the people of this region,

367
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
chasing jobs and opportunity

368
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:31,000
and sue many of our citizens to other states.

369
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
This is a sad narrative that's got to stop.

370
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:37,000
We can no longer have the business climate that we have

371
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
and have a sustainable future.

372
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
You know, the things that my opponent mentions

373
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:44,000
about pharmacies and nursing homes close,

374
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
they're not closing in other states,

375
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:49,000
so I don't believe that the aim should be looking

376
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:50,000
at the federal government

377
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:53,000
because they're surviving and thriving elsewhere.

378
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:57,000
New York has had a long period of economic malaise

379
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:01,000
and we need relief from Kathy Hochul's reign.

380
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:04,000
I have to say, with regard to pharmacies,

381
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
that's not accurate, Congressman.

382
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:09,000
A lot of those closures were due to the bankruptcy of Rite Aid,

383
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:12,000
and the Rite Aid bankruptcy was national.

384
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:18,000
There is disruption in the healthcare markets nationwide.

385
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
Things are not going well,

386
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:23,000
and they're about to get dramatically worse.

387
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:26,000
Arnett Ogden, the hospital here in Elmira,

388
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:30,000
is the largest employer in the city, 2,200 people,

389
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:34,000
and it is the most threatened hospital by the big, ugly bill.

390
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:35,000
It's amazing.

391
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
I visited Arnett many times.

392
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
I talked to their executives on a very regular basis.

393
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:42,000
I'm currently working on getting federal funds

394
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
for projects within Arnett,

395
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
and they're going to benefit directly

396
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
from the Rural Transformation Fund,

397
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:51,000
a $50 billion once of a first time of its kind,

398
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
a fund just aimed at rural healthcare

399
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
all the way across our district,

400
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:56,000
and the plans are already in place

401
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:59,000
to bring those funding pots here to our district

402
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:02,000
in rural hospitals all over America.

403
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
The trail of tears, economically,

404
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:07,000
that we have in this region is because Kathy Hochul

405
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
and the New York State government is out of touch.

406
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:12,000
You know, I agree that our utility bills are too high,

407
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:16,000
and I do blame Albany for that, in part, at least.

408
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
But let me say this.

409
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
The Rural Healthcare Transformation Fund,

410
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:24,000
which is fine, our share of it is $74 million.

411
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:27,000
The big, ugly bill cuts $63 billion

412
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:30,000
out of New York State's Medicare and Medicaid budget.

413
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
It's almost 1,000 times more.

414
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:35,000
So for Congressman Langworthy to compare the two,

415
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:38,000
they're just not comparable.

416
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:39,000
Do I get another rebuttal?

417
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:40,000
Absolutely.

418
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:43,000
I mean, we will spend more at the end of the decade on Medicaid,

419
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:46,000
$45 billion more a year than we spend today.

420
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:49,000
Medicaid will continue to grow and go up.

421
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:53,000
The trajectory of the program was unsustainable.

422
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:56,000
What you have is Medicaid that's been abused

423
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
by states in particular,

424
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
and we have put a common sense work requirement on here

425
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:03,000
because that program must be there for the most vulnerable,

426
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:06,000
the disabled, the impoverished, and our elderly people.

427
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
It's not a lifestyle choice.

428
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:11,000
I'll never apologize for supporting work requirements.

429
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:14,000
All right, Representative Langworthy, this is yours first.

430
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:18,000
A lot of American goods have gone up in price,

431
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
likely because of diesel fuel prices.

432
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
Diesel powers railroads.

433
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:23,000
It powers semi-trucks.

434
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:27,000
It powers a lot of the vehicles that deliver goods to our stores.

435
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:32,000
Should gasoline, diesel fuel, and other products be regulated by law?

436
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:36,000
Well, I think, you know, I don't believe in price controls,

437
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:39,000
but I do think that we need to harness more of our American energy

438
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:42,000
and refine it here in the United States of America.

439
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:45,000
I have long had a problem with refiners,

440
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:48,000
is we have a disparity in price between the Southern Tier

441
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
and the Buffalo metropolitan area in price,

442
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:53,000
even though they're closer to the Pennsylvania refineries.

443
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:57,000
We can solve that problem if we do create a larger refining network

444
00:20:57,000 --> 00:21:02,000
here in the country as we become a greater global exporter

445
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:06,000
of so many of our energy sources.

446
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:09,000
But the fact that we haven't created a new refinery in 50 years,

447
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:12,000
that is something that has got to change.

448
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:15,000
We have to have a broader network of those refineries

449
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:18,000
in order to bring that product to market.

450
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
Dr. Beas?

451
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:24,000
Increasing global exports of liquefied natural gas

452
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:28,000
would not bring down prices in Western New York and the Southern Tier.

453
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:31,000
They would make a scarce commodity more scarce.

454
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
And the more dependent we are on that commodity,

455
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
the higher the price is going to be.

456
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:38,000
This is why I oppose Nick Langworthy's signature piece of legislation,

457
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:42,000
the Energy Choice Act, because by banning the bans, as he said,

458
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:46,000
it would force consumers to pay natural gas prices forever,

459
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:49,000
whatever the gas company wants to charge for them.

460
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:53,000
I am for giving people more control over where they get their energy

461
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:56,000
and how much they pay for it.

462
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:59,000
And I believe to do that we need to diversify the sources

463
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:04,000
from which we get our energy and not give more power to big refiners

464
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:07,000
that are subject to shocks like the current shock

465
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:11,000
and the Strait of Hormuz and the Strait of Bab al-Mandeb and in Ukraine.

466
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:15,000
Well, there's a clear contrast between my opponent and I.

467
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,000
I mean, you heard it from him.

468
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:20,000
He opposes the Energy Choice Act, which would stop Kathy Hochul

469
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:24,000
from banning us from bringing natural gas into our homes.

470
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:28,000
He believes that you should be mandated to be in an all-electric home.

471
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:29,000
I don't.

472
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
I think that it's a matter of life and death

473
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:35,000
for us to have redundancy in the way we heat our homes

474
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
in this very difficult climate.

475
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:41,000
I also believe that the state's climate law is too aggressive,

476
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:45,000
and I will never support things like bans on wood-burning stoves

477
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:48,000
or propane or no more hookups for natural gas.

478
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
But that should be dealt with at the state level.

479
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:53,000
We do not need a federal ban that would prohibit states

480
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:56,000
from doing anything to diversify our energy mix.

481
00:22:56,000 --> 00:23:00,000
That is not the way to make energy affordable and accessible for everyone,

482
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:03,000
which is what we need to do desperately right now

483
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:06,000
when people's electric bills are going up through the roof.

484
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
Okay, I want to ask a follow-up question here.

485
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:11,000
If the state, you say keep it at the state level,

486
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:14,000
but if the state mandates only one form of power,

487
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
is that a good or a bad thing?

488
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:17,000
That would be a bad thing.

489
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:20,000
We need a diversified energy mix.

490
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:21,000
Yes?

491
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:27,000
I think that my opponent's mistaken about the intention of New York State's laws.

492
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:31,000
New York State looks to take us off of natural gas and propane.

493
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:34,000
And yes, there are proposals in New York to ban wood heat.

494
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:38,000
These are lifeblood necessities for people in western New York

495
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:39,000
in the southern tier.

496
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:43,000
When I went to Congress, I introduced this bill out of necessity

497
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:47,000
because I had to find a federal solution around a really bad piece of state policy

498
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:52,000
drawn by New York City residents that don't understand our lifestyle.

499
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:57,000
I mean, Winter Storm Elliott, we lost 69 lives in western New York

500
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:59,000
in that horrible blizzard.

501
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:02,000
It was the most desperate situation I have personally lived through.

502
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:03,000
911 collapsed.

503
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:05,000
People froze to death in their homes.

504
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:09,000
Those were the people that had baseboard electric heat only.

505
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:14,000
The winds were so great that the power lines weren't able to be restored for days.

506
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:18,000
I mean, that should never happen in a place like New York.

507
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:22,000
Unfortunately, these wrongheaded policies that have come out of the New York State Legislature,

508
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:26,000
which has gone to the extreme left in their desire to be the most green,

509
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:29,000
are putting New Yorkers' lives in danger.

510
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:33,000
Doctor, I'm going to come to you with the next question because it's right along the same track here.

511
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:37,000
Are you concerned at all about the American electric grid?

512
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:40,000
Just keep it hyper-local with New York, the New York electric grid,

513
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:44,000
its ability to handle the load that's currently on it,

514
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:47,000
and the fact that grid owners around the country, frankly,

515
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:53,000
have not done much to upgrade their security or their ability to carry more electricity to people.

516
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:54,000
Does that concern you?

517
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:59,000
Yes, I am concerned about that, Jim, and there are several reasons why.

518
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:03,000
One reason is because we have utility companies that are owned by foreign countries,

519
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:08,000
not owned by New York State, and not owned by private companies that are owned right here inside the state.

520
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:13,000
And so they take their profits overseas, and they make their investments, well,

521
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:16,000
based on what they can negotiate with the utility commission.

522
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:20,000
And I don't think we always get the best deal, and I'm frustrated, frankly, with Albany about that.

523
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:24,000
But I'll tell you something else that concerns me, and that is data centers.

524
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:30,000
AI data centers, the number in the pipeline, are poised to double the power demand

525
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:34,000
of every man, woman, and child in business that is currently here in New York State.

526
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:38,000
Our electric grid absolutely cannot support that.

527
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:44,000
And if you don't think that it's going to affect your electricity rates, boy, you've got another thing coming.

528
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
I support the governor's moratorium.

529
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:51,000
And by the way, Nick Langworthy has said this is a local issue, which I think makes no sense.

530
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:56,000
Bruce Blakeman has been very positive about data centers, and so has Donald Trump.

531
00:25:56,000 --> 00:26:01,000
Same question to you about the security of electric grids and their ability to handle the load.

532
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:03,000
I'm deeply concerned about the grid.

533
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:09,000
And, you know, Governor Hochul has been raising rates in a backdoor fashion.

534
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:12,000
She has this faceless commission that raises rates.

535
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
It's not, you know, the governor or the legislature.

536
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:19,000
But they've done it 48 times, and they've done it so that they wouldn't have their fingerprints on it.

537
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:24,000
And they've done it in the name of full electrification, which I believe, as I stated in my previous question,

538
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:27,000
is dangerous for New Yorkers that live in this climate.

539
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:34,000
You know, my opponent talks about data centers, and I did something about that, you know, not just talk.

540
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:38,000
The Rape Payer Protection Act addresses this directly.

541
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
And data centers are coming to the United States.

542
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
They need to generate their own power.

543
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:48,000
And in doing so, they can actually add power back onto the grid rather than take it off.

544
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:53,000
That is a mandate that I think has to be put on these projects so that local people don't have

545
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:57,000
sky-high energy bills because a data center has been cited in their area.

546
00:26:57,000 --> 00:26:59,000
Let's be clear.

547
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:03,000
Congressman Langworthy wants data centers to generate their own power.

548
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:09,000
And that kind of power demand, 500 megawatts for the stream data center in Genesee County,

549
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:12,000
that's a quarter of the Niagara Power Station.

550
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:18,000
How do you generate power like that and do it 30, 40, 70 times, depending on how many data centers actually get built?

551
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:19,000
I'll tell you what you do.

552
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:22,000
You build new nuclear and you frack.

553
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:24,000
I am not for either of those options.

554
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:26,000
We need to generate our own power in New York State.

555
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:31,000
We need a diverse energy mix, and we need to put our residents first, not data centers.

556
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:39,000
I don't quite understand what a diverse energy mix is if you're against a new nuclear power plant in New York,

557
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:41,000
and you're against natural gas.

558
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:47,000
I don't really – I'm not gathering where you think this energy comes from other than the wall socket.

559
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:50,000
I mean, we have to create new energy.

560
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:53,000
And without those two sources, it's not a possibility.

561
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:56,000
The pinwheels and the solar farms aren't cutting it.

562
00:27:56,000 --> 00:28:01,000
I mean, it's going to be no more than 20 percent of our state's needs at any given time.

563
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:02,000
And that is a matter of fact.

564
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:05,000
You could talk to any energy expert about that.

565
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:07,000
That's misinformation.

566
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:08,000
It's not.

567
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:10,000
That's misinformation, yes.

568
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:20,000
There are countries that are generating significantly in excess of 20 percent from renewables, from just solar and wind.

569
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:23,000
But we should open the aperture on renewables a little bit.

570
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:30,000
The Great Lakes have one of the largest potentials in the world for tidal energy, energy that does not hurt fish or spoil your view.

571
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:34,000
We have a great potential here in this district for enhanced geothermal energy.

572
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:38,000
That is U-shaped holes dug in the ground that give you heat forever.

573
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:40,000
Now, you talk about the blizzard.

574
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:45,000
We need a diversified way to heat our homes that doesn't cost us a lot of money.

575
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:49,000
I mean, I think the contrast between us couldn't be more clear on this issue.

576
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:54,000
If you like big industrial solar farms coming into our agricultural territory, he's your guy.

577
00:28:54,000 --> 00:29:03,000
If you want safeguards in place against these green new energy scams coming in and gobbling up our agricultural territory, then I'm your guy.

578
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:05,000
That's a clear difference.

579
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:10,000
These things aren't profitable without the tax subsidies, and they haven't worked anywhere they've been trying.

580
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:12,000
All right, folks, we're going to have to take a break here.

581
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:17,000
So we'll be back to resume this debate, and we'll move into another area of conversation when we come back.

582
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:29,000
Welcome back to our debate.

583
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:30,000
We're going to get right back to the questions.

584
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:32,000
Dr. Geese, you're first on this one.

585
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:36,000
For years, immigration has been an issue where U.S. voters seem to be divided.

586
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:44,000
We recently had a poll done for 18 News and for our sister Nexstar stations that put immigration at a top five issue in this year's campaign.

587
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:49,000
Would you change our country's current approach to immigration enforcement, or would you stay the course?

588
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:51,000
I absolutely would, Jim.

589
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:53,000
I would change it dramatically.

590
00:29:53,000 --> 00:30:01,000
I think that ICE is in some cases terrorizing our communities, and it certainly made the border with Canada

591
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:03,000
a more dangerous place to be.

592
00:30:03,000 --> 00:30:09,000
But we should take a step back, and instead of talking about immigration enforcement, let's talk about our immigration loss,

593
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:13,000
because I think Americans agree that we need comprehensive immigration reform.

594
00:30:13,000 --> 00:30:17,000
I talk to conservatives, and they tell me, I just want people to come here the right way.

595
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:18,000
Well, currently, you can't.

596
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:25,000
You can't come here the right way unless you are a doctor on a J-1 visa and you have money for the extra $100,000 fee,

597
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:27,000
or you're a white South African.

598
00:30:27,000 --> 00:30:33,000
We're not letting any religious persecution refugees into the country right now for the first time in our history.

599
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:38,000
And the Republican Party that's arguably controlled all three branches of the federal government now for two years,

600
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:42,000
they haven't even tried to propose comprehensive immigration reform.

601
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:45,000
We knew what to do about this 40 years ago.

602
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:53,000
We have to get together the political will to fix our broken immigration system and have a clear path for folks to come and for folks that are already here.

603
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:59,000
We had an invasion of our southern border for four years, and it was on purpose.

604
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:05,000
Our federal government opened the border and let 10 million plus, maybe more, across that border.

605
00:31:05,000 --> 00:31:07,000
We had people come that were on the terrorist watch list.

606
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:10,000
We had people wandering around the interior of the country.

607
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:13,000
We don't know where they are or what they've done.

608
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:19,000
The deportation process that's going on right now should always be under review.

609
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:22,000
Any police tactic should always be subject to review.

610
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:23,000
I believe in body cameras.

611
00:31:23,000 --> 00:31:25,000
I think those are things that keep everybody honest.

612
00:31:25,000 --> 00:31:34,000
And most law enforcement officers you talk to relish them after the fact because nine times out of 10, it proves that they did the right thing despite things.

613
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:39,000
But Barack Obama actually deported more people than President Trump has.

614
00:31:39,000 --> 00:31:43,000
But you have a dangerous criminal element just down the road in Endicott, New York.

615
00:31:43,000 --> 00:31:45,000
We had a Peruvian serial killer.

616
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:48,000
Someone so heinous they killed 23 people in their own country.

617
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:53,000
They had their names and tattoos in the faces tattooed on this body.

618
00:31:53,000 --> 00:32:00,000
That person was apprehended by ICE, a menace to society that absolutely could have killed again.

619
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:06,000
If I may, we should always have strong borders and strong immigration enforcement.

620
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:09,000
However, that doesn't require that we terrorize our neighbors.

621
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:12,000
Batavia, ICE's detention facility, is overcrowded.

622
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:18,000
They're expanding it right now, but it's still going to be almost double the capacity that it's supposed to have.

623
00:32:18,000 --> 00:32:22,000
And that impinges on people's human dignity and their human rights.

624
00:32:22,000 --> 00:32:27,000
They have deported folks in this district who have been here for 20 years, who raised families here,

625
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:33,000
who were valued members of their Baptist church, and who were here, in fact, because they processed it for democracy in China.

626
00:32:33,000 --> 00:32:40,000
I mean, due to Kathy Hochul's sanctuary state policies, we have become a destination for illegal immigration.

627
00:32:41,000 --> 00:32:44,000
In New York, they give you housing, they give you food, they give you a cell phone.

628
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:50,000
They take better care of you than they would take care of people that lost their job and that are tax paying New Yorkers.

629
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:58,000
Illegal immigration ran amok in the last four years, and it has to come to a stop in all sanctuary state and city policies.

630
00:32:58,000 --> 00:32:59,000
It must be abolished.

631
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:01,000
Okay, I'm going to keep going down the New York route here.

632
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:03,000
Congressman Langworthy, you're next.

633
00:33:03,000 --> 00:33:06,000
Should state or local government be able to overrule a federal mandate?

634
00:33:06,000 --> 00:33:13,000
Specifically, I'm referring to Governor Hochul and State Attorney General James ordering state law enforcement to refuse to work with ICE.

635
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:16,000
No, I think it's extraordinarily dangerous what they've done.

636
00:33:16,000 --> 00:33:18,000
I mean, you look at the governor's green light law.

637
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:21,000
She wanted to give driver's licenses to illegal immigrants.

638
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:22,000
What did they do?

639
00:33:22,000 --> 00:33:27,000
They banned our local law enforcement from accessing New York state DMV database.

640
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:34,000
That very case I just talked to in the last question at Endicott, it took the ICE officials two extra months to track down that heinous killer,

641
00:33:34,000 --> 00:33:37,000
because they couldn't access that database.

642
00:33:37,000 --> 00:33:42,000
Yet we freely share that same database with the country of Canada at our borders.

643
00:33:42,000 --> 00:33:43,000
That is abhorrent.

644
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:46,000
We have one United States of America.

645
00:33:46,000 --> 00:33:51,000
We are all states within it, and they should not contradict the laws of the federal government.

646
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:58,000
Okay, Dr. Geese, President Trump has said he's not in favor of government regulation of artificial intelligence.

647
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:07,000
In your opinion, is unencumbered growth of AI a danger to the American people, and if so, should it be regulated, and who should be in charge of regulating it?

648
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:10,000
Yes, it absolutely should be regulated.

649
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:18,000
Like any new technology, any new powerful technology, AI has a path that can go towards the good, or it could do great harm.

650
00:34:18,000 --> 00:34:22,000
And we have a responsibility to make sure that it does good.

651
00:34:22,000 --> 00:34:36,000
If we just pursue profit, if we just pursue the potential of the technology, we're headed towards a world where none of us has any privacy, where none of us can control our likeness, where we're surveilled all the time, and where AI defense technologies have the potential for doing great damage.

652
00:34:36,000 --> 00:34:42,000
And so I think it's very important that AI be regulated for the common good with human values forward.

653
00:34:43,000 --> 00:34:53,000
And I think Congress has the primary responsibility to do that regulating, you know, in dialogue with the rest of the world, which also has to deal with the emergence of AI, particularly with China.

654
00:34:53,000 --> 00:35:10,000
Now, when we had – let me say this – Mike Johnson sent Congress home right after we found out that anthropic had dealt with terrifying AI breakouts instead of taking up the issue.

655
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:13,000
It was a dereliction of duty.

656
00:35:13,000 --> 00:35:16,000
AI is something – this is an area where I disagree with President Trump.

657
00:35:16,000 --> 00:35:22,000
We do not need just a presidential dictate on AI.

658
00:35:22,000 --> 00:35:30,000
We shouldn't, you know, have the tech billionaires decide their regulation and stifle any innovation that comes from any would-be competitors.

659
00:35:30,000 --> 00:35:36,000
Congress has to do its job and do exactly that, and it actually is in the process of doing its job.

660
00:35:37,000 --> 00:35:50,000
The last time that a potentially dangerous technology like this was invented, it was the nuclear bomb.

661
00:35:50,000 --> 00:36:05,000
And after that, we had a very serious 30, 40-year conversation between avowed enemies, the U.S. and the U.S.S.R., that led to the I.C.A.

662
00:36:06,000 --> 00:36:08,000
It led to arms reduction.

663
00:36:08,000 --> 00:36:12,000
It led to peaceful nuclear power and nonproliferation.

664
00:36:12,000 --> 00:36:20,000
That conversation is not happening internationally right now between the U.S. and China and our partners in the U.N., and I blame President Trump for that.

665
00:36:20,000 --> 00:36:22,000
So I agree with Congressman Langworthy.

666
00:36:22,000 --> 00:36:24,000
Congress has the responsibility here.

667
00:36:24,000 --> 00:36:31,000
Congressman Langworthy, New York pays nearly $32,000 in taxes for each public school student.

668
00:36:31,000 --> 00:36:33,000
That's the highest in the nation.

669
00:36:33,000 --> 00:36:36,000
The national average is a little under $18,000.

670
00:36:36,000 --> 00:36:45,000
Despite that, ratings on America's Dashboard.com show that New York ranks 30th nationally, 24th in graduation rate, 27th in 8th grade math scores.

671
00:36:45,000 --> 00:36:50,000
Is there anything that members of Congress can do to help this back up?

672
00:36:50,000 --> 00:36:57,000
I mean, our rate per pupil is unacceptable for the outcomes, especially in our inner-city schools.

673
00:36:58,000 --> 00:37:12,000
I mean, we have pushed an opportunity for New York State to opt into a school choice voucher system that would allow people to have a choice if you have failing schools and allow them to have tax credits for that.

674
00:37:12,000 --> 00:37:18,000
I think that it, especially for our inner-city kids, they deserve far much better.

675
00:37:18,000 --> 00:37:27,000
But the State Education Department over-regulates the system, and, you know, additional federal regulations, I don't believe, will help the outcomes.

676
00:37:27,000 --> 00:37:29,000
Dr. Giese?

677
00:37:29,000 --> 00:37:32,000
So I haven't had a chance to say it here yet, but I'm an educator.

678
00:37:32,000 --> 00:37:35,000
I'm a professor at St. Bonaventure University.

679
00:37:35,000 --> 00:37:36,000
That's my career.

680
00:37:36,000 --> 00:37:38,000
And I came from a working-class family.

681
00:37:38,000 --> 00:37:42,000
Neither my mom nor my dad went to college.

682
00:37:42,000 --> 00:37:45,000
But, you know, they worked hard, and they did well.

683
00:37:45,000 --> 00:37:49,000
We were the kind of family that, you know, they didn't fly for vacations, but we weren't hungry either.

684
00:37:49,000 --> 00:37:51,000
And they're very proud of my education.

685
00:37:51,000 --> 00:37:54,000
And excellence was really important to them, as it is to me.

686
00:37:54,000 --> 00:38:02,000
I do not believe that we should be trying to dismantle the Department of Education, as Linda McMahon, President Trump's Secretary of Education, is doing.

687
00:38:02,000 --> 00:38:13,000
I think that we do have a responsibility to make sure that we have common standards nationally, although we should never determine curriculum.

688
00:38:13,000 --> 00:38:21,000
But, you know, another thing that I'd like to point out is that the cuts to Medicaid and the big, ugly bill represent a horrible disinvestment in our children's education.

689
00:38:21,000 --> 00:38:27,000
Because those funds fund special education, individual education plans, and school lunches.

690
00:38:27,000 --> 00:38:34,000
And the biggest cause of learning loss in the American school child's day is hunger.

691
00:38:34,000 --> 00:38:37,000
Dr. Giese, I want to get you first on this one.

692
00:38:37,000 --> 00:38:42,000
New York is getting rid of Regents exams as graduation requirements starting next school year.

693
00:38:42,000 --> 00:38:46,000
Is that a good or a bad idea?

694
00:38:46,000 --> 00:38:50,000
Well, I believe that the Teachers Union supports the idea.

695
00:38:50,000 --> 00:38:55,000
I have been endorsed by NYCID, and I'm very proud to be endorsed by our teachers.

696
00:38:55,000 --> 00:39:02,000
I have not had a chance to review the standards with which they will be replacing the Regents exam.

697
00:39:02,000 --> 00:39:05,000
The Regents exam is not very old, right?

698
00:39:05,000 --> 00:39:08,000
It's something that we've been doing for a couple of decades.

699
00:39:08,000 --> 00:39:13,000
But testing standards can change as research standards change.

700
00:39:13,000 --> 00:39:30,000
We just need to make sure that we are evaluating our students in a way that encourages them to achieve excellence and obtain the skills that they need to compete in tomorrow's workplace.

701
00:39:30,000 --> 00:39:31,000
Congressman?

702
00:39:32,000 --> 00:39:42,000
I have no jurisdiction over the matter of the Regents exam, but I think that it pretty much says to the world that we're not going to strive for excellence anymore.

703
00:39:42,000 --> 00:39:47,000
And I think we should always be trying to challenge our students to be at their highest possible standard.

704
00:39:47,000 --> 00:39:50,000
And that Regents diploma, that used to mean something.

705
00:39:50,000 --> 00:39:58,000
I remember getting my Regents diploma growing up here, and it was a matter of pride for my parents.

706
00:39:58,000 --> 00:40:02,000
Also, you know, I come from a working class background. My mother was a factory worker.

707
00:40:02,000 --> 00:40:07,000
My father run a small business for, you know, 48 years now.

708
00:40:07,000 --> 00:40:13,000
And we should always be pushing for the highest standards we possibly can for our kids.

709
00:40:13,000 --> 00:40:17,000
And just eliminating this in a blanket fashion doesn't make sense to me.

710
00:40:17,000 --> 00:40:21,000
All right, we've got to take a commercial break, and we'll be back in just a couple of minutes to wrap up the debate.

711
00:40:21,000 --> 00:40:24,000
This is our last commercial break. Hope you'll stick around.

712
00:40:24,000 --> 00:40:26,000
It goes very fast.

713
00:40:27,000 --> 00:40:32,000
Live from your local election headquarters, this is debate night in New York.

714
00:40:35,000 --> 00:40:38,000
Welcome back to our congressional debate. We're going to get right to the questions.

715
00:40:38,000 --> 00:40:40,000
Congressman Langworthy, this is for you.

716
00:40:40,000 --> 00:40:45,000
Should the U.S. minimum wage get raised, and if you think so, how much?

717
00:40:45,000 --> 00:40:48,000
I think that the minimum wage question has been answered by the states.

718
00:40:48,000 --> 00:40:52,000
I mean, the state of New York has increased its minimum wage greatly,

719
00:40:52,000 --> 00:40:58,000
and I think it is better suited to find the cost of living in a particular area.

720
00:40:58,000 --> 00:41:04,000
I mean, New York State, you know, obviously went very far when we were one of the first states to go to a $15 minimum wage.

721
00:41:04,000 --> 00:41:12,000
I've heard a lot from businesses that arbitrary wage hikes by the state of New York have been drastically impactful on business.

722
00:41:12,000 --> 00:41:17,000
And if you, you know, the federal minimum wage, I think, is very antiquated,

723
00:41:17,000 --> 00:41:22,000
because if you tried to hire someone at that number, you're not going to find anyone to answer the call.

724
00:41:22,000 --> 00:41:26,000
So at this point, I think the minimum wage question is best left to the states.

725
00:41:26,000 --> 00:41:28,000
Dr. Giese?

726
00:41:28,000 --> 00:41:40,000
I support the Raise the Wage Act, which would raise the federal minimum wage to $15 immediately and then move towards $18.

727
00:41:40,000 --> 00:41:42,000
There are a couple of reasons for that.

728
00:41:42,000 --> 00:41:46,000
The one is states that border us that have the federal minimum wage,

729
00:41:46,000 --> 00:41:57,000
they really do eat our lunch economically when folks can jump over the border and earn a New York wage that's much more close, close to a living wage.

730
00:41:57,000 --> 00:42:08,000
But another reason is because the federal minimum wage is not something that anyone can live on, and that hasn't been true for decades now.

731
00:42:08,000 --> 00:42:15,000
I earned the minimum wage when it was first moved to $7.25 an hour when I was just out of high school.

732
00:42:15,000 --> 00:42:19,000
It's ridiculous that we haven't moved it at all that time.

733
00:42:19,000 --> 00:42:22,000
Speaking of which, no tax on tips, no tax on overtime.

734
00:42:22,000 --> 00:42:24,000
These are good things, and we should keep them.

735
00:42:24,000 --> 00:42:34,000
But ultimately, we need to be pushing for folks to earn a living wage so that they don't have to rely on tips and overtime just to get by.

736
00:42:34,000 --> 00:42:36,000
Dr. Giese, the next question is for you.

737
00:42:36,000 --> 00:42:40,000
The U.S. will pay around $1 trillion interest on our national debt this year.

738
00:42:40,000 --> 00:42:42,000
Despite that, government spending keeps growing.

739
00:42:42,000 --> 00:42:46,000
How concerned should people be about the national debt and why?

740
00:42:46,000 --> 00:42:47,000
Yes.

741
00:42:47,000 --> 00:42:54,000
Someone pointed out to me in an email today that our national debt will grow by a billion dollars during the time this debate is going on.

742
00:42:54,000 --> 00:43:06,000
It's deeply concerning, deeply unsettling, I would even say, that our national debt has gotten so high and now one out of every $3 we send to the IRS goes just to pay the interest on our national credit card.

743
00:43:06,000 --> 00:43:09,000
We have to tame the debt, and we should be clear.

744
00:43:09,000 --> 00:43:18,000
President Trump owns over half of that debt through bills that he passed with Congress with the help, in the second case, of Congressman Langworthy.

745
00:43:18,000 --> 00:43:27,000
These unfunded, irresponsible tax cuts to billionaires and corporations have led to this debt, as have forever wars, including the war in Iran.

746
00:43:27,000 --> 00:43:33,000
Now, to fix the problem, job one is to get the rich to pay their fair share of taxes.

747
00:43:33,000 --> 00:43:44,000
We have a very regressive tax code that taxes working people, taxes the middle class, and allows the ultra-wealthy, in many cases, to pay a lower effective tax rate, and that's not right.

748
00:43:44,000 --> 00:43:47,000
I'm deeply concerned about our debt.

749
00:43:47,000 --> 00:43:51,000
I have taken steps to address our deficit.

750
00:43:51,000 --> 00:43:53,000
I mean, we've put rescissions packages forward.

751
00:43:53,000 --> 00:44:01,000
The federal government is not in the business of a lot of things that it was before President Trump took office in my time in Congress.

752
00:44:02,000 --> 00:44:11,000
You know, my opponent runs on a platform of one-size-fits-all government healthcare, which comes with a $34 trillion bill.

753
00:44:11,000 --> 00:44:15,000
I mean, that's not the way you grow the economy or shrink the debt.

754
00:44:15,000 --> 00:44:19,000
That's piling on new government programs, new government debts, and forever entitlements.

755
00:44:19,000 --> 00:44:25,000
75% of all spending is mandatory in this country, and it is not sustainable.

756
00:44:25,000 --> 00:44:30,000
Universal single-payer healthcare is the way you grow the economy and shrink the debt.

757
00:44:30,000 --> 00:44:37,000
There's a study this summer that showed that if we got it right, the government could save a trillion dollars a year, a trillion dollars a year.

758
00:44:37,000 --> 00:44:40,000
Now, that's not $40 trillion, but it's a start.

759
00:44:40,000 --> 00:44:52,000
And think about the weight that that takes off of small business, takes off of farmers not to have to deal with the benefits costs of covering employees' health insurance through W-2 taxes.

760
00:44:52,000 --> 00:45:01,000
This relieves the pressure from the self-employed, stops people from having to work jobs they hate just to keep the health insurance.

761
00:45:01,000 --> 00:45:11,000
I mean, single-payer health insurance, it eliminates choice, it eliminates opportunities, it eliminates innovation, and if you enjoy your experience at the DMV, you would love it.

762
00:45:11,000 --> 00:45:16,000
It is not something that American people will believe in rationed care.

763
00:45:16,000 --> 00:45:26,000
I mean, the idea that a government bureaucrat is going to make a decision whether or not your relative is worthy of the state-of-the-art treatment to fight their cancer or the knee replacement that they might need.

764
00:45:26,000 --> 00:45:36,000
I mean, these are the sorts of trap doors that come with one-size-fits-all government healthcare, and that's why you see so many Canadian license plates still in our MRI centers.

765
00:45:36,000 --> 00:45:44,000
When we choose not to provide healthcare for folks with universal single-payer healthcare, we are choosing to ration healthcare based on who can pay for it.

766
00:45:44,000 --> 00:45:56,000
And I just do not believe that Americans believe we should decide who lives or dies of a preventable disease based on whether they are poor or whether they live in a rural area that can't sustain a full-service hospital.

767
00:45:56,000 --> 00:46:03,000
I don't think that's the best we can do in the richest country in the world, and I don't think Americans are satisfied with that, and they shouldn't be.

768
00:46:03,000 --> 00:46:11,000
Congressman Langworthy, New York's comptroller said last week that the state's going to be in the red by more than $3 billion this year.

769
00:46:11,000 --> 00:46:17,000
And the reason I ask this question, he blames the federal government for not sending more tax dollars to the state.

770
00:46:17,000 --> 00:46:24,000
He also reports the state could be more than $30 billion in the red in three years, which could mean higher taxes or program cuts.

771
00:46:24,000 --> 00:46:28,000
Do you agree with the comptroller that it's about federal tax dollars' income?

772
00:46:28,000 --> 00:46:30,000
I don't. I think New York has a priorities problem.

773
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:40,000
I mean, we have spent $8 billion to care for illegal immigrants that we basically asked to come to the state under Kathy Hochul's watch.

774
00:46:40,000 --> 00:46:43,000
I mean, that is not a respectful program.

775
00:46:43,000 --> 00:46:48,000
You have the governor proposing billions of dollars for free health care for sex workers.

776
00:46:48,000 --> 00:46:55,000
I mean, these are the sorts of things that aren't on the ballot this year but are, as you have to make your decision as to who you want to lead this state.

777
00:46:55,000 --> 00:47:00,000
The government of New York has a priorities problem and not a revenue problem.

778
00:47:00,000 --> 00:47:03,000
$277 billion was this year's budget.

779
00:47:03,000 --> 00:47:07,000
When Andrew Cuomo left office, it was $185 billion.

780
00:47:07,000 --> 00:47:10,000
I mean, we are spending extraordinarily more sums of money.

781
00:47:10,000 --> 00:47:20,000
They just have to dial in as to absolutely what our needs are in this state and understand, you know, more taxes and more fees are chasing more of the people that pay those taxes away.

782
00:47:20,000 --> 00:47:21,000
Dr. Giese?

783
00:47:21,000 --> 00:47:25,000
Well, let's talk about the kinds of things that we've defunded in this state at the federal level.

784
00:47:25,000 --> 00:47:31,000
First of all and foremost, we have defunded our health care, our health care.

785
00:47:31,000 --> 00:47:37,000
New York 23 is the most hurt by the big, ugly bills, Medicare and Medicaid cuts of any district in the state.

786
00:47:37,000 --> 00:47:40,000
And we are the second most hurt state in the nation.

787
00:47:40,000 --> 00:47:45,000
So this district is right in the crosshairs of President Trump's cuts.

788
00:47:45,000 --> 00:47:48,000
President Trump is defunding the police with Nick Langworthy's connivance.

789
00:47:48,000 --> 00:47:59,000
He has withheld $79 million that was intended to train sheriff's departments and other law enforcement through the Doge process.

790
00:47:59,000 --> 00:48:04,000
And while we're talking about Doge, Doge has hurt veterans.

791
00:48:04,000 --> 00:48:05,000
It's hurt our Social Security system.

792
00:48:05,000 --> 00:48:09,000
It has hurt grants for research.

793
00:48:09,000 --> 00:48:11,000
It has hurt universities.

794
00:48:11,000 --> 00:48:13,000
It's hurt the postal system.

795
00:48:13,000 --> 00:48:25,000
And Langworthy was one of the founding members of the Congressional Doge Caucus who cheered on all these arbitrary destructive cuts that ultimately didn't save us any money.

796
00:48:26,000 --> 00:48:30,000
My opponent has pointed out Doge is a process.

797
00:48:30,000 --> 00:48:40,000
And the funny thing about when Doge started, the outrage at the outset was ridiculous because we wanted to see accountability in government.

798
00:48:40,000 --> 00:48:43,000
We wanted to look under the hood and see what these departments were spending money on.

799
00:48:43,000 --> 00:48:53,000
USAID was social engineering around the globe, spending money on sex changes in South America, interfering in elections throughout the globe.

800
00:48:53,000 --> 00:48:55,000
That was on the back of the U.S. taxpayer.

801
00:48:55,000 --> 00:49:01,000
And neither Congress, probably the executive at that time, nor the American people knew what they were spending their money on.

802
00:49:01,000 --> 00:49:11,000
An estimated 700,000 African children born with HIV have died because of the arbitrary and illegal closure of USAID.

803
00:49:11,000 --> 00:49:16,000
I think USAID might have performed some kind of like 20 gender-affirming surgeries.

804
00:49:16,000 --> 00:49:18,000
Come on. Come on.

805
00:49:18,000 --> 00:49:20,000
And we took USAID out of Africa.

806
00:49:20,000 --> 00:49:24,000
And what has filled the power vacuum for American soft power?

807
00:49:24,000 --> 00:49:32,000
Now that there's no HIV drugs or peanut butter available to stop the famine, Islamist militias are filling the power vacuum.

808
00:49:32,000 --> 00:49:35,000
If we do not use soft power, we will need hard power.

809
00:49:35,000 --> 00:49:37,000
It is absolutely ludicrous.

810
00:49:37,000 --> 00:49:40,000
The PEPFAR funds are still in place and have been delivered.

811
00:49:40,000 --> 00:49:46,000
USAID, as an agency, was dismantled because it was mismanaged and misleading the government.

812
00:49:46,000 --> 00:49:51,000
Right now, we have put it under the State Department and it will continue to deliver its work.

813
00:49:51,000 --> 00:49:53,000
It should have required an act of Congress.

814
00:49:53,000 --> 00:49:56,000
Okay, we're going to now make 30-second answers because we're running out of time here.

815
00:49:56,000 --> 00:49:58,000
So, let's talk with you, Dr. Geese.

816
00:49:58,000 --> 00:49:59,000
Okay.

817
00:49:59,000 --> 00:50:09,000
Do you think social media platforms should be held accountable or responsible for harm done to minors who are approached by disreputable people or who are exposed to advertising for products that might harm them?

818
00:50:10,000 --> 00:50:12,000
Yes, I absolutely do.

819
00:50:12,000 --> 00:50:16,000
And, you know, there's a larger, like, common good problem here.

820
00:50:16,000 --> 00:50:23,000
There are something like eight people who control the algorithms that control all of our social media.

821
00:50:23,000 --> 00:50:26,000
And those algorithms are black boxes.

822
00:50:26,000 --> 00:50:29,000
They are as secret as the recipe for Coca-Cola.

823
00:50:29,000 --> 00:50:42,000
And if you're a parent that has watched a child, like, slowly lose their self-esteem and lose their joy in life because they're seeing disturbing images that they want to compete with on Instagram, then you know what that's like.

824
00:50:43,000 --> 00:50:44,000
Congressman.

825
00:50:44,000 --> 00:50:46,000
I definitely think that these companies should be held accountable.

826
00:50:46,000 --> 00:50:52,000
They should be held to the highest standard and we should have protections against our young people on social media platforms.

827
00:50:52,000 --> 00:50:57,000
It could be far too poisonous to a young person's mind to be on these platforms at too early of an age.

828
00:50:57,000 --> 00:50:59,000
Congressman, I'll go to you first on this one.

829
00:50:59,000 --> 00:51:03,000
Some in Congress are talking about passing a bill that would establish a four-day work week.

830
00:51:03,000 --> 00:51:07,000
And some of the Congressmen are saying it should be four, eight-hour days.

831
00:51:07,000 --> 00:51:08,000
What's your reaction?

832
00:51:08,000 --> 00:51:09,000
I believe that's ludicrous.

833
00:51:09,000 --> 00:51:13,000
I mean, we need to have the most productive country possible.

834
00:51:13,000 --> 00:51:16,000
And the five-day work week is something that should remain.

835
00:51:16,000 --> 00:51:17,000
Dr. Geese?

836
00:51:17,000 --> 00:51:21,000
Well, I believe that people should have opportunity in America.

837
00:51:21,000 --> 00:51:24,000
And that means that if you want to work extra hours, you should be able to.

838
00:51:24,000 --> 00:51:29,000
Congress shouldn't pass a law that stops you from working a five-day week.

839
00:51:29,000 --> 00:51:37,000
On the other hand, we have to look at ways to raise wages, right, to expand small business opportunity,

840
00:51:37,000 --> 00:51:46,000
and to level the playing field for small businesses against the anti-competitive practices of the richest corporations and monopolies.

841
00:51:46,000 --> 00:51:48,000
Dr. Geese, this one's for you first.

842
00:51:48,000 --> 00:51:53,000
Do you agree with heavier taxes on wealthier New Yorkers than those paid by people with lower incomes?

843
00:51:54,000 --> 00:51:59,000
I don't believe that this is a state issue, right?

844
00:51:59,000 --> 00:52:06,000
So nationally, I do believe that we have a tax code that allows the wealthy to get away with not paying their fair share.

845
00:52:06,000 --> 00:52:13,000
And we all suffer from that in terms of the federal government not being able to meet its budget fund, its programs.

846
00:52:13,000 --> 00:52:20,000
We need a more progressive tax code that causes the ultra-wealthy to pay an effective tax rate that's similar to the one that you and I pay.

847
00:52:21,000 --> 00:52:30,000
I mean, New York State, under Kathy Hochul, and with the pressure that's now on her from the leader of the Democratic Party in New York State, Zoran Mandami,

848
00:52:30,000 --> 00:52:33,000
we are going to see an eat-the-rich mentality.

849
00:52:33,000 --> 00:52:37,000
They are going to continue to chase Wall Street out of New York City.

850
00:52:37,000 --> 00:52:41,000
Our state is deeply dependent on Wall Street revenue to balance its books.

851
00:52:41,000 --> 00:52:45,000
As we, you know, post-COVID, people know they can do their work from anywhere in the world.

852
00:52:45,000 --> 00:52:47,000
The technology exists. They can do it.

853
00:52:47,000 --> 00:52:54,000
And they are, as Texas has hired more bankers at the big banks than New York State has at this point.

854
00:52:54,000 --> 00:52:55,000
That's dangerous.

855
00:52:55,000 --> 00:52:57,000
Okay, 20 second answers.

856
00:52:57,000 --> 00:53:00,000
What's your opinion about the states that redrew their congressional districts this year?

857
00:53:00,000 --> 00:53:05,000
I believe that redistricting should be done once a decade.

858
00:53:05,000 --> 00:53:07,000
Dr. Gies?

859
00:53:07,000 --> 00:53:08,000
I agree.

860
00:53:08,000 --> 00:53:14,000
I would love to see national fair maps legislation that would stop states from gerrymandering altogether.

861
00:53:14,000 --> 00:53:18,000
I think that our congressional districts need to be fair and they need to be tight,

862
00:53:18,000 --> 00:53:21,000
and we shouldn't have politicians choosing their voters.

863
00:53:21,000 --> 00:53:23,000
The voters should choose their politicians.

864
00:53:23,000 --> 00:53:25,000
All right, gentlemen, we're about to run out of time.

865
00:53:25,000 --> 00:53:29,000
I want to thank both of you for what I think was a very substantive debate.

866
00:53:29,000 --> 00:53:30,000
Thank you for your answers.

867
00:53:30,000 --> 00:53:32,000
Thank you for the way you approached tonight.

868
00:53:32,000 --> 00:53:33,000
Very cordial between two of you.

869
00:53:33,000 --> 00:53:35,000
You exchanged strong opinions.

870
00:53:35,000 --> 00:53:37,000
That's what this is supposed to be about.

871
00:53:37,000 --> 00:53:40,000
So thank you very much for being here, and we're going to sign off now.

872
00:53:40,000 --> 00:53:41,000
I'll send you to NBC.

873
00:53:41,000 --> 00:53:43,000
Thank you for being here, everybody, for this debate.

874
00:53:43,000 --> 00:53:45,000
Hope you'll tune in the next time we do one.

875
00:53:45,000 --> 00:53:47,000
Thank you.

876
00:53:47,000 --> 00:53:48,000
Thank you.

877
00:53:48,000 --> 00:53:49,000
Thanks for the time.

878
00:54:13,000 --> 00:54:15,000
Thank you.

